          </g>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div class="legend">
        <span><i class="swatch sw12"></i>positive</span>
        <span><i class="swatch swg"></i>negative / GND</span>
        <span><i class="swatch sw33"></i>control / 3.3 V</span>
        <span>● junction — crossing without a dot is <em>not</em> connected</span>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        The two suppressors are drawn as avalanche diodes (the bent cathode bar) because that is what they
        are: reverse-biased and non-conducting until the rail exceeds their breakdown. The bulk capacitor is
        drawn polarised — straight plate positive, curved plate negative.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">DRAWING 06</span>Terminal-block view</h2>
    <p>The same circuit as Drawing 05, redrawn the way it is physically built: as blocks landing on two
       terminal strips. Use this one at the bench for routing and labelling —
       <strong>X1</strong> for the battery domain, <strong>X2</strong> for the switched sensor domain.</p>

    <figure>
      <div class="canvas">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 500" role="img"
             aria-label="Detailed power schematic. Solar panel positive goes through a TVS node to the charge controller, then to the battery, then in series through a PTC fuse and an SS34 diode to the protected VBATT node. VBATT feeds the WaziSense board VIN and the XL6009 boost converter, whose output passes through a high-side switch and a 1000 microfarad capacitor to the plus 12 volt sensor rail. Every negative connection drops to one common ground rail at the bottom.">
          <g font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor">

            <!-- ===== row 1: generation + protection ===== -->
            <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none">
              <rect x="30"  y="72" width="100" height="58" rx="2"/>
              <rect x="200" y="72" width="120" height="58" rx="2"/>
              <rect x="390" y="72" width="100" height="58" rx="2"/>
            </g>
            <text x="80"  y="96"  text-anchor="middle">Solar</text>
            <text x="80"  y="112" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">6 V 5–10 W</text>
            <text x="260" y="96"  text-anchor="middle">Charge ctrl</text>
            <text x="260" y="112" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">MPPT</text>
            <text x="440" y="96"  text-anchor="middle">Battery</text>
            <text x="440" y="112" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">18650 1–2× ∥</text>

            <!-- + path row 1 -->
            <g stroke="var(--rail12)" stroke-width="2">
              <line x1="130" y1="101" x2="200" y2="101"/>
              <line x1="320" y1="101" x2="390" y2="101"/>
              <line x1="490" y1="101" x2="530" y2="101"/>
              <line x1="584" y1="101" x2="600" y2="101"/>
              <line x1="654" y1="101" x2="712" y2="101"/>
            </g>
            <text x="152" y="94" fill="var(--rail12)">+ SOL</text>
            <text x="336" y="94" fill="var(--rail12)">+ BAT</text>

            <!-- PTC + SS34 in series -->
            <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" fill="var(--surface)">
              <rect x="530" y="88" width="54" height="26" rx="2"/>
              <rect x="600" y="88" width="54" height="26" rx="2"/>
            </g>
            <text x="557" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">PTC</text>
            <text x="627" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">SS34</text>
            <text x="557" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">2.6 A</text>
            <text x="627" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">rev.-pol.</text>

            <!-- VBATT node -->
            <circle cx="712" cy="101" r="3.5" fill="var(--rail12)"/>
            <text x="722" y="94" fill="var(--rail12)">+VBATT  (X1-1)</text>

            <!-- TVS drops -->
            <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" fill="var(--surface)">
              <rect x="152" y="140" width="26" height="24" rx="2"/>
              <rect x="676" y="140" width="26" height="24" rx="2"/>
            </g>
            <line x1="165" y1="101" x2="165" y2="140" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
            <line x1="165" y1="164" x2="165" y2="440" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
            <circle cx="165" cy="101" r="3" fill="var(--rail12)"/>
            <text x="184" y="156" font-size="10">TVS solar</text>
            <line x1="689" y1="101" x2="689" y2="140" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
            <line x1="689" y1="164" x2="689" y2="440" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
            <circle cx="689" cy="101" r="3" fill="var(--rail12)"/>
            <text x="708" y="156" font-size="10">SMBJ5.0A</text>

            <!-- − drops from row 1 -->
            <g stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.6">
              <line x1="80"  y1="130" x2="80"  y2="440"/>
              <line x1="260" y1="130" x2="260" y2="440"/>
              <line x1="440" y1="130" x2="440" y2="440"/>
            </g>
            <text x="86"  y="150" fill="var(--ink-3)">− SOL</text>
            <text x="266" y="150" fill="var(--ink-3)">−</text>
            <text x="446" y="150" fill="var(--ink-3)">− BAT</text>

            <!-- ===== branch to board ===== -->
            <line x1="712" y1="101" x2="712" y2="196" stroke="var(--rail12)" stroke-width="2"/>
            <line x1="712" y1="196" x2="770" y2="196" stroke="var(--rail12)" stroke-width="2"/>
            <circle cx="712" cy="196" r="3.5" fill="var(--rail12)"/>
            <rect x="770" y="168" width="180" height="86" rx="2" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/>
            <text x="786" y="190" font-family="IBM Plex Sans Condensed, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">WaziSenseV2</text>
            <text x="786" y="210" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">VIN · 3V3 out · D4 · A0</text>
            <text x="758" y="192" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--rail12)" font-size="10">VIN</text>
            <line x1="860" y1="254" x2="860" y2="440" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
            <text x="866" y="274" fill="var(--ink-3)">− GND</text>
            <!-- 3V3 out -->
            <line x1="950" y1="228" x2="972" y2="228" stroke="var(--rail33)" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="978" y="224" fill="var(--rail33)" font-size="10" text-anchor="end">+3V3</text>
            <text x="978" y="238" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10" text-anchor="end">→ X1-3</text>

            <!-- ===== row 3: boost + switch + 12 V rail ===== -->
            <path d="M 712 196 L 712 268 L 100 268 L 100 330" stroke="var(--rail12)" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>
            <text x="360" y="262" fill="var(--rail12)">+VBATT to boost</text>

            <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none">
              <rect x="100" y="302" width="120" height="56" rx="2"/>
              <rect x="288" y="302" width="130" height="56" rx="2"/>
            </g>
            <text x="160" y="326" text-anchor="middle">XL6009</text>
            <text x="160" y="342" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">3.7 → 12 V</text>
            <text x="353" y="326" text-anchor="middle">High-side sw</text>
            <text x="353" y="342" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">Pololu #2811</text>

            <g stroke="var(--rail12)" stroke-width="2">
              <line x1="220" y1="330" x2="288" y2="330"/>
              <line x1="418" y1="330" x2="500" y2="330"/>
              <line x1="500" y1="330" x2="950" y2="330"/>
            </g>
            <text x="228" y="322" fill="var(--rail12)" font-size="10">+12 V raw</text>
            <circle cx="500" cy="330" r="3.5" fill="var(--rail12)"/>
            <text x="640" y="322" fill="var(--rail12)">+12 V switched  (X2-1)  →  probes</text>

            <!-- bulk cap -->
            <line x1="560" y1="330" x2="560" y2="362" stroke="var(--rail12)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
            <circle cx="560" cy="330" r="3" fill="var(--rail12)"/>
            <g stroke="var(--rail12)" stroke-width="2">
              <line x1="542" y1="362" x2="578" y2="362"/>
              <line x1="542" y1="370" x2="578" y2="370"/>
            </g>
            <line x1="560" y1="370" x2="560" y2="440" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
            <text x="586" y="360" font-size="10">1000 µF</text>
            <text x="586" y="374" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">watch polarity</text>

            <!-- − drops row 3 -->
            <g stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.6">
              <line x1="140" y1="358" x2="140" y2="440"/>
              <line x1="330" y1="358" x2="330" y2="440"/>
            </g>
            <text x="146" y="378" fill="var(--ink-3)">−</text>
            <text x="336" y="378" fill="var(--ink-3)">−</text>

            <!-- gate control D4 -->
            <path d="M 800 254 L 800 286 L 384 286 L 384 302" stroke="var(--rail33)" stroke-width="1.6"
                  stroke-dasharray="5 4" fill="none"/>
            <text x="500" y="280" fill="var(--rail33)" font-size="10">D6 → ON</text>
            <circle cx="620" cy="286" r="3" fill="var(--rail33)"/>
            <rect x="607" y="306" width="26" height="24" rx="2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" fill="var(--surface)"/>
            <line x1="620" y1="286" x2="620" y2="306" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
            <line x1="620" y1="330" x2="620" y2="440" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
            <text x="640" y="322" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10"> </text>
            <text x="562" y="322" font-size="10">100 kΩ</text>
            <text x="530" y="308" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">pull-down</text>

            <!-- ===== GND rail ===== -->
            <line x1="30" y1="440" x2="960" y2="440" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="3"/>
            <text x="30" y="466" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="11">GND / X1-2 — one common node. Star-point it at the battery minus, not daisy-chained.</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div class="legend">
        <span><i class="swatch sw12"></i>positive (+)</span>
        <span><i class="swatch swg"></i>negative (−) / GND</span>
        <span><i class="swatch sw33"></i>3.3 V / control</span>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Series order on the positive side is fixed: <b>battery → PTC → SS34 → VBATT</b>. The PTC sits first so
        it protects everything behind it; the TVS sits after the diode so it clamps what reaches the boost
        input. The <b>100 kΩ pull-down</b> on the gate line is not optional — without it the rail state is
        undefined between power-up and the first firmware instruction.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">DRAWING 07</span>Sensor bus fan-out — X2</h2>
    <p>Each RS485 probe takes <strong>four separate conductors</strong>: two for power, two for data. All eight
       land on the same four terminals of block X2.</p>

    <figure>
      <div class="canvas">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 400" role="img"
             aria-label="Terminal block X2 has four terminals: plus 12 volt switched, ground, RS485 A and RS485 B. Eight wires fan out from these four terminals, four to each of the two probes: dissolved oxygen and Y511-A turbidity. The PT1000 is not on this bus. No termination resistor is fitted at the probes; the single 120 ohm terminator sits at the transceiver.">
          <g font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor">

            <!-- terminal block X2 -->
            <rect x="60" y="60" width="150" height="240" rx="2" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/>
            <text x="135" y="48" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Sans Condensed, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">Terminal block X2</text>

            <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.1" fill="none">
              <rect x="76" y="80"  width="118" height="44" rx="2"/>
              <rect x="76" y="134" width="118" height="44" rx="2"/>
              <rect x="76" y="188" width="118" height="44" rx="2"/>
              <rect x="76" y="242" width="118" height="44" rx="2"/>
            </g>
            <text x="88" y="107" fill="var(--rail12)">X2-1  +12 V</text>
            <text x="88" y="161" fill="var(--ink-3)">X2-2  GND</text>
            <text x="88" y="215" fill="var(--data)">X2-3  A</text>
            <text x="88" y="269" fill="var(--data)">X2-4  B</text>

            <!-- probe boxes -->
            <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none">
              <rect x="700" y="56" width="230" height="66" rx="2"/>
              <rect x="700" y="152" width="230" height="66" rx="2"/>
            </g>
            <text x="716" y="80">DO — DFRobot SEN0680</text>
            <text x="716" y="98" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">addr 0x02 · 10–30 V</text>
            <text x="716" y="110" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">V+ · V− · A · B</text>
            <text x="716" y="176">Y511-A turbidity</text>
            <text x="716" y="194" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">addr 0x03 · 5–12 V · wiper</text>
            <text x="716" y="206" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">V+ · V− · A · B</text>
            <text x="700" y="272" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">PT1000 is not on this bus —</text>
            <text x="700" y="286" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">bare 3-wire RTD via MAX31865 on SPI (Table 07b)</text>

            <!-- 4 wires x 2 probes -->
            <!-- +12 V -->
            <g stroke="var(--rail12)" stroke-width="1.8" fill="none">
              <path d="M 194 96 L 300 96 L 300 68 L 700 68"/>
              <path d="M 194 100 L 320 100 L 320 164 L 700 164"/>
            </g>
            <!-- GND -->
            <g stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.8" fill="none">
              <path d="M 194 150 L 380 150 L 380 82 L 700 82"/>
              <path d="M 194 154 L 400 154 L 400 178 L 700 178"/>
            </g>
            <!-- A -->
            <g stroke="var(--data)" stroke-width="1.8" fill="none">
              <path d="M 194 204 L 460 204 L 460 96 L 700 96"/>
              <path d="M 194 208 L 480 208 L 480 192 L 700 192"/>
            </g>
            <!-- B -->
            <g stroke="var(--data)" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-dasharray="7 3" fill="none">
              <path d="M 194 258 L 540 258 L 540 110 L 700 110"/>
              <path d="M 194 262 L 560 262 L 560 206 L 700 206"/>
            </g>

            <text x="300" y="46" fill="var(--rail12)" font-size="10">2 × V+</text>
            <text x="380" y="130" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">2 × V−</text>
            <text x="462" y="240" fill="var(--data)" font-size="10">2 × A</text>
            <text x="546" y="336" fill="var(--data)" font-size="10">2 × B (dashed)</text>

            <!-- probes are deliberately unterminated -->
            <text x="930" y="252" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">no terminator at the probes —</text>
            <text x="930" y="264" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">the single 120 Ω sits at the transceiver</text>

            <text x="60" y="370" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="11">Probe lead colours are vendor-specific — always confirm against the probe's own datasheet before crimping.</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div class="legend">
        <span><i class="swatch sw12"></i>V+ (12 V)</span>
        <span><i class="swatch swg"></i>V− (GND)</span>
        <span><i class="swatch swd"></i>RS485 A / B</span>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Eight conductors total from X2 to the probes. <b>No terminator goes at the probes</b> — the single
        120 Ω sits at the transceiver (Drawing 02). Because the probe leads are moulded pigtails they are
        electrically stubs whichever way you route them; at 9600 baud that is harmless, so bring them all
        back to X2 in the dry enclosure rather than building a junction near the water.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">TABLE 03</span>Netlist — power generation and protection</h2>
    <p>Terminal blocks: <strong>X1</strong> = battery domain (1 = +VBATT, 2 = GND, 3 = +3V3),
       <strong>X2</strong> = switched sensor domain (1 = +12 V, 2 = GND, 3 = A, 4 = B).</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Net</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Wire</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>SOL+</code></td><td>Solar panel <b>+</b></td><td>Charge controller <code>SOLAR+</code></td><td>red 0.75 mm²</td><td>TVS to GND at this node</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>SOL−</code></td><td>Solar panel <b>−</b></td><td>Charge controller <code>SOLAR−</code></td><td>black 0.75 mm²</td><td>common with GND</td></tr>
          <tr><td>—</td><td>TVS cathode (banded end)</td><td><code>SOL+</code></td><td>—</td><td>anode → GND</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>BAT+</code></td><td>Charge controller <code>BAT+</code></td><td>Battery pack <b>+</b></td><td>red 0.75 mm²</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>BAT−</code></td><td>Charge controller <code>BAT−</code></td><td>Battery pack <b>−</b></td><td>black 0.75 mm²</td><td>= GND star point</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>VB1</code></td><td>Battery <b>+</b></td><td>PTC terminal 1</td><td>red</td><td>PTC first in the chain</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>VB2</code></td><td>PTC terminal 2</td><td>SS34 <b>anode</b></td><td>red</td><td>diode direction matters</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>VBATT</code></td><td>SS34 <b>cathode</b> (banded)</td><td><code>X1-1</code></td><td>red</td><td>protected rail</td></tr>
          <tr><td>—</td><td>SMBJ5.0A cathode</td><td><code>X1-1</code></td><td>—</td><td>anode → <code>X1-2</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td>Battery <b>−</b></td><td><code>X1-2</code></td><td>black</td><td>single star point</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">TABLE 04</span>Netlist — board and 3.3 V domain</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Net</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Wire</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>VBATT</code></td><td><code>X1-1</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>VIN</code></td><td>red</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td><code>X1-2</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>GND</code></td><td>black</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>3V3</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>3V3</code> out</td><td><code>X1-3</code></td><td>orange</td><td>supplies EZO isolators, MAX3485, DS18B20</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>VSENSE</code></td><td>— nothing to wire —</td><td>WaziSense <code>A0</code></td><td>—</td><td><b>on-board</b> 2 × 470 kΩ divider (<code>BATT%</code> jumper). Do not add your own.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ANT</code></td><td>WaziSense antenna port</td><td>SMA bulkhead → λ/2 dipole</td><td>coax pigtail</td><td>seal the outside joint with self-amalgamating tape</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">TABLE 05</span>Netlist — 12 V rail and gating</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Net</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Wire</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>VBATT</code></td><td><code>X1-1</code></td><td>XL6009 <code>IN+</code></td><td>red</td><td>confirm the module accepts 3 V input</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td><code>X1-2</code></td><td>XL6009 <code>IN−</code></td><td>black</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>V12RAW</code></td><td>XL6009 <code>OUT+</code></td><td>Switch <code>VIN</code></td><td>red</td><td>set trimpot to 12 V before connecting probes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td><code>X1-2</code></td><td>Switch <code>GND</code></td><td>black</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>V12SW</code></td><td>Switch <code>VOUT</code></td><td><code>X2-1</code></td><td>red</td><td>the switched rail</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td><code>X1-2</code></td><td><code>X2-2</code></td><td>black</td><td>ground is <em>never</em> switched</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CAP+</code></td><td>1000 µF <b>+</b></td><td><code>X2-1</code></td><td>red</td><td>polarity — reversed electrolytics vent</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CAP−</code></td><td>1000 µF <b>−</b></td><td><code>X2-2</code></td><td>black</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RAIL_EN</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>D6</code></td><td>"Sensor Power 1" — on-board MOSFET</td><td>—</td><td>Internal to the board. No external gate, no pull-down needed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td colspan="5"><b>H1 = BATT variant:</b> feed <code>XL6009 IN+/IN−</code> from the "Sensor Power 1" terminal <b>+</b>/<b>−</b> instead of directly from <code>X1-1</code>/<code>X1-2</code>. The converter is then gated too and its quiescent current disappears. <b>H1 = 3.3 V variant:</b> keep the external 22 A high-side module in the 12 V line and drive its control input from <code>D6</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">TABLE 06</span>Netlist — RS485 transceiver</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Net</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Wire</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>RS_RO</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>D3</code></td><td>MAX3485 <code>RO</code></td><td>green</td><td>SoftwareSerial RX</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RS_DI</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>D4</code></td><td>MAX3485 <code>DI</code></td><td>blue</td><td>SoftwareSerial TX</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RS_DE</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>D5</code></td><td>MAX3485 <code>DE</code></td><td>white</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RS_DE</code></td><td>MAX3485 <code>DE</code></td><td>MAX3485 <code>RE</code></td><td>jumper</td><td>tie together — one pin drives both</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>3V3</code></td><td><code>X1-3</code></td><td>MAX3485 <code>VCC</code></td><td>orange</td><td>3.3 V part, not the 5 V MAX485</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td><code>X1-2</code></td><td>MAX3485 <code>GND</code></td><td>black</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RS485_A</code></td><td>MAX3485 <code>A</code></td><td><code>X2-3</code></td><td>yellow</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RS485_B</code></td><td>MAX3485 <code>B</code></td><td><code>X2-4</code></td><td>green</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>—</td><td>120 Ω</td><td><code>X2-3</code> ↔ <code>X2-4</code></td><td>—</td><td><b>the only terminator</b> — none at the probes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>—</td><td>680 Ω</td><td><code>X2-3</code> → <code>X1-3</code></td><td>—</td><td>fail-safe bias, pull-up — matched to a single 120 Ω</td></tr>
          <tr><td>—</td><td>680 Ω</td><td><code>X2-4</code> → <code>X1-2</code></td><td>—</td><td>fail-safe bias, pull-down — gives 268 mV idle</td></tr>
          <tr><td>—</td><td>Cable shield</td><td>Enclosure ground</td><td>drain wire</td><td><b>enclosure end only</b>, far end open</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">TABLE 07</span>Netlist — probes on the bus</h2>
    <p>Repeat these four wires for <strong>each</strong> of the two RS485 probes — eight conductors in total.
       The PT1000 is <strong>no longer on this bus</strong> (see Table 07b).</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Net</th><th>From</th><th>To — DO 0x02</th><th>To — Y511-A 0x03</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>V12SW</code></td><td><code>X2-1</code></td><td>probe <code>V+</code></td><td>probe <code>V+</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td><code>X2-2</code></td><td>probe <code>V−</code></td><td>probe <code>V−</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RS485_A</code></td><td><code>X2-3</code></td><td>probe <code>A</code></td><td>probe <code>A</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RS485_B</code></td><td><code>X2-4</code></td><td>probe <code>B</code></td><td>probe <code>B</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>—</td><td>termination</td><td colspan="2"><b>none at the probes</b> — the single 120 Ω sits at the transceiver (Table 06)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">TABLE 07b</span>Netlist — PT1000 via MAX31865 (SPI)</h2>
    <p>The probe supplied is a <strong>bare 3-wire RTD</strong> (2 × red + 1 × white), not a Modbus device, so it
       gets its own front-end on the SPI bus. The RTD itself needs no supply — it is a resistor.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Net</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Wire</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>3V3</code></td><td><code>X1-3</code></td><td>MAX31865 <code>VIN</code></td><td>orange</td><td>3.3 V part, matches WaziSense logic</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td><code>X1-2</code></td><td>MAX31865 <code>GND</code></td><td>black</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>SCK</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>D13</code></td><td>MAX31865 <code>CLK</code></td><td>short lead</td><td>shared with the radio</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>MISO</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>D12</code></td><td>MAX31865 <code>SDO</code></td><td>short lead</td><td>shared with the radio</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>MOSI</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>D11</code></td><td>MAX31865 <code>SDI</code></td><td>short lead</td><td>shared with the radio</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RTD_CS</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>A1</code></td><td>MAX31865 <code>CS</code></td><td>white</td><td>its own chip select</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RTD_A</code></td><td>PT1000 <b>red</b></td><td>terminal at the paired end (1 of 2)</td><td>—</td><td rowspan="3">Set the breakout's solder jumper to <b>3-wire</b>. The two same-colour leads go to the two terminals at one end, the odd-coloured lead to the other.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RTD_A2</code></td><td>PT1000 <b>red</b></td><td>terminal at the paired end (2 of 2)</td><td>—</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RTD_B</code></td><td>PT1000 <b>white</b></td><td>terminal at the single end</td><td>—</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note">
      <h3>Two ways to get zero readings out of a perfectly good RTD</h3>
      <p><strong>Reference resistor.</strong> <code>RREF</code> must be <strong>4300 Ω</strong> for a PT1000. Breakouts sold
         for PT100 carry <strong>430 Ω</strong> — with that fitted the numbers are wrong by roughly a factor of ten
         and no amount of software will fix it. Either buy the PT1000 variant or swap the resistor, and set
         <code>RREF 4300.0</code> / <code>RNOMINAL 1000.0</code> in the sketch to match.</p>
      <p><strong>Shared SPI, different modes.</strong> The RFM95W runs SPI mode 0, the MAX31865 mode 1, on the same
         three wires. The Adafruit driver wraps its access in an SPI transaction so the two coexist — but if
         temperatures come back as nonsense <em>while LoRa still works</em>, a stuck SPI mode is the first thing
         to suspect. Verify with the multimeter first: <b>red ↔ red ≈ 0 Ω</b>, <b>red ↔ white ≈ 1078 Ω at 20 °C</b>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">TABLE 08</span>Netlist — I²C, isolators and EZO circuits</h2>
    <p>Wire this block <strong>twice</strong> — once for pH, once for EC. Each isolator creates its own floating
       supply and ground.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Net</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Wire</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>3V3</code></td><td><code>X1-3</code></td><td>Isolator <code>VCC</code> (MCU side)</td><td>orange</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td><code>X1-2</code></td><td>Isolator <code>GND</code> (MCU side)</td><td>black</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>SDA</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>A4</code></td><td>Isolator <code>SDA</code> (MCU side)</td><td>blue</td><td>both isolators share this net</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>SCL</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>A5</code></td><td>Isolator <code>SCL</code> (MCU side)</td><td>white</td><td>both isolators share this net</td></tr>
          <tr><td>—</td><td colspan="4"><b>I²C pull-ups: nothing to fit.</b> WaziSense v2 already carries 2 × 4.7 kΩ to 3.3 V, enabled by the <code>I2C_J</code> jumper (closed from the factory). Adding your own in parallel gives ~2.35 kΩ and overloads the bus.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ISO_VCC</code></td><td>Isolator <code>VCC</code> (isolated side)</td><td>EZO <code>VCC</code></td><td>short lead</td><td>floating supply</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ISO_GND</code></td><td>Isolator <code>GND</code> (isolated side)</td><td>EZO <code>GND</code></td><td>short lead</td><td><b>must not touch <code>X1-2</code></b></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ISO_SDA</code></td><td>Isolator <code>SDA</code> (isolated side)</td><td>EZO <code>SDA</code></td><td>short lead</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ISO_SCL</code></td><td>Isolator <code>SCL</code> (isolated side)</td><td>EZO <code>SCL</code></td><td>short lead</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>PH_IN</code></td><td>pH probe BNC centre</td><td>EZO-pH probe <code>IN</code></td><td>coax core</td><td>keep this lead short</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>PH_SHD</code></td><td>pH probe BNC shell</td><td>EZO-pH probe <code>GND</code></td><td>coax shield</td><td>isolated domain, not <code>X1-2</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>EC_1</code></td><td>EC probe conductor 1</td><td>EZO-EC probe pin 1</td><td>—</td><td>EC probe leads are not polarised</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>EC_2</code></td><td>EC probe conductor 2</td><td>EZO-EC probe pin 2</td><td>—</td><td></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">TABLE 09</span>Netlist — DS18B20 backup (optional)</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Net</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Wire</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>3V3</code></td><td><code>X1-3</code></td><td>DS18B20 <code>VDD</code></td><td>red</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td><code>X1-2</code></td><td>DS18B20 <code>GND</code></td><td>black</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>OW_DATA</code></td><td>WaziSense <code>A2</code></td><td>DS18B20 <code>DQ</code></td><td>yellow</td><td>D9 is the radio's RESET — not usable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>—</td><td>4.7 kΩ</td><td><code>OW_DATA</code> → <code>X1-3</code></td><td>—</td><td>mandatory 1-Wire pull-up — this one you <em>do</em> fit</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>The isolated grounds are not grounds</h3>
    <p>Each EZO circuit sits behind its own isolator, so <code>ISO_GND</code> for pH and <code>ISO_GND</code> for
       EC are <strong>two separate floating nets</strong> — neither of them is <code>X1-2</code>. Connecting either one
       to the main ground, or to each other, restores exactly the path the isolators exist to break, and the pH
       reading will start drifting whenever EC measures. When you check continuity during assembly, an open
       circuit between <code>ISO_GND</code> and <code>X1-2</code> is the <em>correct</em> result.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">NOTES</span>Before you power it up</h2>
    <ul class="checks">
      <li><span class="n">01</span><div><strong>Set the RS485 addresses one probe at a time.</strong> All three ship from
        the factory on the same default address. Connect a single probe, set its address, disconnect,
        repeat. Two devices answering at once produces corrupted frames that look like a wiring fault.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">02</span><div><strong>Switch +12 V, never ground.</strong> The two RS485 probes share their
        ground with the transceiver as the bus reference. Gating the ground side breaks that reference
        and the bus fails in ways that are hard to trace.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">03</span><div><strong>Confirm the XL6009 input range.</strong> Some variants start at 5 V, not 3 V.
        You are feeding it from a 3.0–4.2 V cell, so verify the low end and set the output trimpot to
        12 V <em>before</em> connecting any probe.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">04</span><div><strong>Isolate pH from EC.</strong> Both electrodes sit in the same water and the EC
        probe injects AC current into it. Without galvanic isolation between the two EZO circuits, the pH
        reading drifts whenever EC measures. Atlas sells isolated carrier boards for exactly this case.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">05</span><div><strong>Ground the cable shield at one end only.</strong> The enclosure end.
        Grounding both ends turns the shield into a ground loop.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">06</span><div><strong>Desolder the module power LEDs.</strong> The boost converter, transceiver and
        load-switch breakouts each burn 1–5 mA continuously. On a solar node, together they can exceed the
        MCU's sleep current.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">07</span><div><strong>Bring the buses up separately.</strong> Use the <code>ENABLE_*</code> flags at the
        top of the sketch to test one sensor at a time before wiring the full bus.</div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Register maps still need verifying</h3>
    <p>The wiring above is settled, but the Modbus <strong>register numbers, data format and scaling</strong> in
       the sketch's <code>MODBUS SENSOR MAP</code> block are placeholders. Check each against its own
       datasheet before trusting a reading. Useful symptom: if a value comes back as nonsense
       <em>while the CRC passes</em>, the register is probably right and the word order is wrong — switch
       that sensor between <code>FMT_FLOAT_ABCD</code> and <code>FMT_FLOAT_CDAB</code>.</p>
  </div>

</div>
